# Answer 2 · Women's Political Participation, Democracy and Development in Pakistan (CSS 2017)
@py question 2017-4

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Accept the statement's logic with evidence, then test it against Pakistan's socio-economic realities, and conclude that participation helps only when it is real.
@ask COMMENT: "Women's ACTIVE POLITICAL PARTICIPATION could STABILISE DEMOCRACY and BOOST ECONOMIC DEVELOPMENT"
@ask within the context of the SOCIO-ECONOMIC REALITIES of Pakistani society
(1) Introduction: the statement holds in principle, but "active" participation is limited in Pakistan
(2) Meaning: participation as voters, candidates, representatives, leaders
(3) Democracy 1: legitimacy and inclusion of half the citizens
(4) Democracy 2: consensus, cross-party work and peace
(5) Development 1: spending on health, education and water
(6) Development 2: women's economic rights and labour-force participation
(7) Pakistani evidence: the caucus laws, BISP, local councillors
(8) Reality 1: poverty, low literacy and the voter gap
(9) Reality 2: patriarchy, feudal and tribal power, dynasties
(10) Reality 3: party dependence, military interventions, weak institutions
(11) Making participation "active": reforms
(12) Conclusion: real participation, not token presence, stabilises and develops
:::

## Decoding the question
**"Comment"** asks for your **judgement** on the statement, with reasons. The statement makes two claims — women's participation stabilises **democracy** and boosts **development** — so test both. **"Within the context of the socio-economic realities of Pakistani society"** means you must show which Pakistani conditions help or limit this potential. Note the word **active**: token presence is not enough.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**Political participation** means taking part in public affairs — voting, campaigning, contesting elections, representing and leading.

#### ii) The argument
The statement is **true in principle and supported by evidence**: women's participation makes democracy more legitimate and inclusive, and directs spending towards health, education and women's work. But in Pakistan, **socio-economic realities** — poverty, patriarchy, feudal power, dynasties and party control — keep women's participation more **nominal** than **active**. The potential will be realised only if participation becomes real.

### 2. What women's participation means in Pakistan
Women are about half of Pakistan's **241 million** people. In ==2024== they were **46.1%** of registered voters, voted at about **43%** against **52%** for men, and won **11** general National Assembly seats, alongside **60** reserved seats. Participation is "**active**" when women vote by their own choice, contest elections, debate and shape policy — not merely when they are counted on the roll or placed on a party list.

### 3. Democracy 1: legitimacy and inclusion
A democracy that excludes half its citizens is incomplete. Women's presence makes parliament more **representative** and more **trusted**, and gives women a stake in the system. The **UDHR** (Article 21) and **CEDAW** (Article 7) treat participation as a right; a state that respects it is more legitimate. When women see themselves represented, they are more likely to trust institutions, use the courts and complain through lawful channels — all signs of a stable democracy.

### 4. Democracy 2: consensus, cross-party work and peace

#### i) Cross-party work
In a polarised parliament, the **Women's Parliamentary Caucus** (==2008==) has been one of the few bodies in which members of all parties cooperate.

#### ii) Peace and stability
Research behind **UN Security Council Resolution 1325** (==2000==) suggests peace agreements last longer when women take part. Women's networks often bridge divides. In Pakistan, women legislators from rival parties have jointly moved bills and resolutions on violence, harassment and inheritance — rare moments of consensus in a confrontational system.

### 5. Development 1: spending priorities
Women representatives tend to give priority to **health, education, water and nutrition** — investments with high economic returns. Healthy, educated mothers raise healthier, better-educated children, so these choices pay off across generations. In India, women village leaders chosen by quota invested more in **drinking water** (Chattopadhyay and Duflo, ==2004==).

### 6. Development 2: women's economic rights
Women's laws help women enter and stay in work: the **workplace harassment law** (==2010==), the **Sindh Home-Based Workers Act** (==2018==), the **Enforcement of Women's Property Rights Act** (==2020==) and maternity leave rules. Female labour-force participation is only ==24.4%== (LFS 2024–25); raising it would add greatly to national income. International studies have estimated that closing gender gaps in work could raise the GDP of countries like Pakistan by a large margin; women legislators have been the main advocates of the laws that make this possible.

::: fig cycle | How active participation by women can strengthen democracy and development.
@centre Women's active participation
Legitimate, inclusive democracy
Cross-party consensus and peace
Spending on health, education, water
Laws for women's work and property
More women in paid work; higher growth
Healthier, educated next generation
:::

### 7. Pakistani evidence
The pro-women laws of ==2010–2016==, passed in assemblies with 60-plus women members, show the **substantive** impact of participation. The **Benazir Income Support Programme** (==2008==), paid to women, was championed by women in government and reached millions of poor households. The women councillors of ==2001== raised water, schools and health in local councils.

#### An example from the assemblies
The Senate bill against honour killings, moved by Senator **Sughra Imam** in ==2014==, shows women legislators acting for women — and for the rule of law. After the murder of **Qandeel Baloch** in ==2016==, women members across parties pressed until a joint sitting passed the law in October. Such cross-party action is exactly the kind of consensus that strengthens democratic institutions.

### 8. Reality 1: poverty, literacy and the voter gap

#### i) Poverty and literacy
Female literacy is about ==53%== (Census 2023). Poor, uneducated women have little time, information or confidence to engage in politics. Many cannot read ballot papers or party manifestos, and depend on male relatives for political information.

#### ii) The voter gap
In ==2024== there were ==9.94 million== fewer women than men on the electoral roll, mainly because many women lack identity cards.

### 9. Reality 2: patriarchy, feudal power and dynasties

#### i) Patriarchal norms
**Purdah** and family **honour** limit women's mobility; many women vote as husbands or elders decide; some local agreements have barred women from voting.

#### ii) Feudal and tribal power
Landlords and *biradari* networks control votes and tickets. In such areas, a woman's vote is often negotiated as part of a family or clan bloc, and women candidates without such backing stand little chance.

#### iii) Dynasties
Many women in politics come from **political families**, so their participation can reinforce elite power rather than open politics to ordinary women.

### 10. Reality 3: parties, interruptions and institutions

#### i) Party dependence
Reserved-seat women are chosen by party leaders and must follow party lines.

#### ii) Military interventions
Democracy itself has been interrupted repeatedly, limiting the growth of stable institutions. Ironically, the two largest expansions of women's seats — ==1985== and ==2002== — came under military rulers, which allowed critics to call them cosmetic.

#### iii) Weak implementation
Laws passed by women are poorly enforced — police, courts and local officials often ignore them. In ==2025== there were **no women federal ministers**, so women had little say over the economic decisions that most affect development.

### 11. Making participation "active"
1. **Register every woman** — free, mobile CNIC drives linked to BISP and health cards; enforce the **10% women's turnout** rule (Elections Act 2017, Section 9).
2. **Direct election** of women's seats and a stronger candidate quota in **winnable** seats.
3. **Party democracy** — women in party executives and ticket committees.
4. **Local government** with **33%** women's seats as a training ground.
5. **Girls' education and women's work** — the socio-economic base of participation.
6. **Safety** — action against harassment and violence, online and offline.
7. **Equal resources** — development funds, offices and committee roles for all women members, so that they can deliver for their voters.

### 12. Conclusion
The statement is right: women's participation can **stabilise democracy** by making it legitimate and consensual, and **boost development** by redirecting resources and protecting women's work. Pakistan's women legislators have already shown this. But in Pakistan's socio-economic reality, participation is often **nominal**. Only when it becomes **active** — independent, resourced and widespread — will its promise be fulfilled. Investing in women's political voice is therefore an investment in both a steadier democracy and a stronger economy.

## Why this answer scores
- Tests **both claims** — democracy and development — with evidence.
- Applies the statement to Pakistan's **socio-economic realities**, as the question requires.
- Picks up the key word **"active"** and builds the argument around it.
- A **cycle figure** shows the causal chain.
